uations $u=\\sqrt 2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c8/c83bd9d913d4959ad275ab4420b647cd030fff45438f59d026e3658bd146a1ee.png)" 13194139534507 17592186045417 false] [17592186198369 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423500 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423498 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423499 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198377 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423496 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198377 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423502 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423497 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198384 1038 #edb/ref 17592186423501 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198481 1050 480.0 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198577 1071 "![](images/1f/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198577 1071 "![](images/1f/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" 13194139534507 17592186045417 false] [17592186198578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198578 1071 "![](images/41/413ff5566eedb8e99c8fb0f3d29b8effd0e146ee4367db93264a5392edd818c1.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T$, given by\n\n$T: (u, v) → (x(u, v), y(u, v))$,\n\nwhere\n\n$x = u\\cos v, y = u\\sin v$,\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D$. Which of the following could be the region $Δ$?" 13194139534507 17592186045417 false] [17592186198578 1071 "![](images/41/413ff5566eedb8e99c8fb0f3d29b8effd0e146ee4367db93264a5392edd818c1.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198578 1074 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b2/b221864b2795f2b158e8c4d1f88da23437f8d043da6c31e0b5c8392ebc1118bd.png)" 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198580 1071 "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186198580 1071 "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" 13194139534507 17592186045417 false] [17592186199463 1064 "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nA sketch of $\\Delta$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n     \n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ onto the $xy$-plane, we need to transform each side (bottom, top, left, and right) of $\\Delta$ separately: \n\n![](images/06/062a3d1f13e8566770118650c92231ebe17f5eba2b4ae8d8b41722e33a72baff.png)\n\nIt can be shown that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$ So, let's proceed to find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by mapping these four sides." 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186199463 1064 "Consider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nA sketch of $\\Delta$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\nTo find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ onto the $xy$-plane, we need to transform each side (bottom, top, left, and right) of $\\Delta$ separately: \n\n![](images/06/062a3d1f13e8566770118650c92231ebe17f5eba2b4ae8d8b41722e33a72baff.png)\n\nIt can be shown that $\\mathbf T$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$ So, let's proceed to find the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by mapping these four sides." 13194139534507 17592186045417 false] [17592186199475 1064 "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)" 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186199475 1064 "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)" 13194139534507 17592186045417 false] [17592186199476 1064 "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the images of the vertical lines:\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e0e5a723b7a9fdc860bcf3f619b6db4bdbec24c06bc22f3121a620080969b6c.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1b9f163724e97d662192d5d274768b3e501843a2a2267adc5a3e1f5c6c9d230.png)\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/04/048606038e26fe79430899c284beff24921397879bb0ce5d46ab1f3653cab47e.png)" 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186199476 1064 "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\nOur transformation $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the images of the vertical lines:\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$ Substituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/2e/2e0e5a723b7a9fdc860bcf3f619b6db4bdbec24c06bc22f3121a620080969b6c.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1b9f163724e97d662192d5d274768b3e501843a2a2267adc5a3e1f5c6c9d230.png)\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/04/048606038e26fe79430899c284beff24921397879bb0ce5d46ab1f3653cab47e.png)" 13194139534507 17592186045417 false] [17592186278345 1071 "![](images/c8/c86259b973101d17d732f4ac8c29a1b61de9fadfc67424a2ab6d28a2ceaa9a01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" 13194139534507 17592186045417 true] [17592186278345 1071 "![](images/c8/c86259b973101d17d732f4ac8c29a1b61de9fadfc67424a2ab6d28a2ceaa9a01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$